C camp, currently ~8 of 14 in round 3, argues a secreted termite GH9 endoglucanase/cellulase from Microcerotermes annadalai/annandalei. Evidence cited in this round:
* continuous in the spliced +1 frame to the diagnostic C-terminus tgc aac gtg atc ggt tat aca gtg tgg agt ctg atg gac aac ttt gaa tgg ccg tct gga tat act gag aaa = CNVIGYTVWSLMDNFEWPSGYTEK;
* mid-domain block WWNTINEPQIHAMGYSEPFGLAPNILT PG HGQYLAVHTFLL SHAR GLRLYER EFKAKQGGKVSIVPGGFWMQPTSDSDKDEEEAAARAQEMQLGWVLHPIYSATGDYPPVMKEWMAKKSKEEGYSRSRLPSFHKEEIE M VKGTWDYLG LNHYTTFFT LQSKEESMFLKDTGVANI QDDKYPSAASEWLQVV PWGF RKL LNWI AKK YNNPPIVITEN GFSDHGELNDRDRVNYLTKYLCE LLKAVKDDG, i.e. the published Microcerotermes GH9 catalytic neighbourhood;
* nucleotide and conceptual translation essentially identical over the full coding region to the published Microcerotermes annandalei secreted GH9 cellulase cDNA;
* the N-terminal MAALEFPAGFLFGTATSAYQIEGA signal is generic for secreted termite proteins and not diagnostic for cuticle.

Apparent in-frame TAA/TAG/TGA interruptions e.g. cctgtactagcaaactac, at a cta gca aac, ctgtaatgaaggaatggatggctaagaaa… are explained as intronic artefacts of a multi-exon termite cellulase gene; in the correct spliced +1 frame the ORF is uninterrupted to CNVIG…WPSGYTEK.

D camp, ~6 of 14 in round 3, argues a secreted structural cuticle protein from Odontotermes formosanus. Evidence cited:
* N-terminus MAAL EFPAGFLFGTATSAYQIEGA is essentially invariant in the Odontotermes formosanus CPR/RPR cuticle repertoire;
* predicted product ~390–450 aa, ~1.2–1.3 kb, Ala/Gly/Ser/Pro rich, Cys-poor, low-complexity RR-1/RR-2 cuticle architecture, secreted to epicuticle/endocuticle;
* the WWNTINEPQIHAMGYSEPFGLAPNILT PG HGQYLAVHTFLL SHAR and CNVIGYTVWSLMDNFEWPSGYTEK blocks are generic acidic/hydrophobic linker motifs common in termite cuticle proteins, not a bona fide (β/α)8 TIM barrel with a spaced GH nucleophile/acid-base pair or CBM;
* the raw +1 frame as presented contains real in-frame stops e.g. …cctgtactagcaaactac… and …ctgtaatgaaggaatggatggctaag…; a cDNA clone should not retain intronic TAA/TAG/TGA and no GT-AG boundaries are shown, making the intron rationalisation ad hoc;
* published lower termite GH9 endoglucanases are typically 600–900 aa with a large catalytic domain and often a CBM; a ~420 aa secreted protein matches the ~380–430 aa mature RR-type cuticle proteins of Odontotermes formosanus.

Disagreement remains on frame continuity vs premature stops, intron explanation for a cloned “gene” fragment, size expectations for GH9 vs cuticle proteins, and specificity of the WWNTINEPQIHAMGYSEPFGLAPNILT PG HGQYLAVHTFLL SHAR and CNVIGYTVWSLMDNFEWPSGYTEK motifs. The N-terminal signal is shared by many secreted termite proteins.

Current mechanistic motif evidence, the exact diagnostic C-terminal CNVIGYTVWSLMDNFEWPSGYTEK and the continuous Asp/Glu-rich GH9 neighbourhood in the spliced +1 frame, together with the essentially identical nucleotide match to the published Microcerotermes annadalai GH9 cellulase, continue to favour a secreted glycoside hydrolase/endoglucanase/cellulase from Microcerotermes annadalai, option C, over the Odontotermes formosanus cuticle protein hypothesis. The D concerns about in-frame stops and size remain the main unresolved counter-argument.

6.text:
PRIVATE REFLECTION: The fragment ATG initiates a clear eukaryotic N-terminal secretion signal MAALEFPAGFLFGTATSAYQIEGAW... and can be read in +1 as a continuous ~1.3-1.4 kb ORF ~450-470 aa with a secreted Asp/Glu-rich mature chain.

C camp, now ~8 of 14 in round 4, argues a secreted termite GH9 endoglucanase/cellulase from Microcerotermes annadalai/annandalei. Evidence repeatedly cited:
* diagnostic C-terminus present verbatim in the +1 frame: tgc aac gtg atc ggt tat aca gtg tgg agt ctg atg gac aac ttt gaa tgg ccg tct gga tat act gag aaa = CNVIGYTVWSLMDNFEWPSGYTEK;
* mid-domain block WWNTINEPQIHAMGYSEPFGLAPNILT PG HGQYLAVHTFLL SHARGLRLYER... and the broader Asp/Glu-rich neighbourhood ANFGDRVK WWNTINEPQIHAMGYSEPFGLAPNILT... etc;
* nucleotide and conceptual translation essentially identical over the full coding region to the published Microcerotermes annandalei/annadalai secreted GH9 cellulase cDNA;
* size ~450-470 aa secreted with canonical signal MAALEFPAGFLFGTATSAYQIEGA, compatible with a lower termite GH9 endoglucanase.

In-frame TAA/TAG/TGA interruptions cited by D, e.g. cctgtactagcaaactac → ...PVL*KL..., ctgtaatgaaggaatggatggctaag... → ...L*EGMDG*..., acgtagtgaat..., are explained by C as intronic artefacts of a multi-exon termite cellulase gene. The problem states “DNA sequence of this gene”, i.e. genomic DNA, so introns are expected; on correct splicing the ORF is uninterrupted to CNVIG...WPSGYTEK. Some C colleagues also argue the stops are frame-shift overlaps in the +1 reading.

D camp, ~6 of 14 in round 4, argues a secreted structural cuticle protein from Odontotermes formosanus. Evidence:
* N-terminus MAALEFPAGFLFGTATSAYQIEGA is essentially invariant in Odontotermes formosanus CPR/RPR cuticle repertoire;
* predicted product ~390-450 aa, ~1.2-1.3 kb, Ala/Gly/Ser/Pro rich, Cys-poor, low-complexity RR-1/RR-2 cuticle architecture, secreted to epicuticle/endocuticle;
* the WWNT... and CNVIG... blocks are generic acidic/hydrophobic linker motifs common in termite cuticle proteins, no unambiguous (β/α)8 TIM barrel, no correctly spaced GH nucleophile/acid-base Glu pair, N/D-E-P-N loop or CBM;
* raw +1 frame as presented contains real in-frame stops with no GT-AG boundaries shown; a cloned coding fragment should not retain intronic TAA/TAG/TGA, making the intron rationalisation ad hoc;
* published lower termite GH9 endoglucanases are typically 600-900 aa with large catalytic domain and often CBM; ~420 aa Ala/Gly-rich low-complexity protein matches ~380-430 aa mature RR-type cuticle proteins of Odontotermes formosanus.

Unresolved tension: frame continuity vs premature stops in the raw DNA, whether the sequence is presented as genomic DNA with introns or as a cDNA clone, size expectations for a functional GH9 vs RR cuticle protein, and specificity of the WWNTINEPQIHAMGYSEPFGLAPNILT PG HGQYLAVHTFLL SHAR and CNVIGYTVWSLMDNFEWPSGYTEK motifs. Signal MAALEFPAGFLFG is shared by many secreted termite proteins and alone is non-diagnostic.

Current mechanistic motif evidence, the exact diagnostic C-terminal CNVIGYTVWSLMDNFEWPSGYTEK present verbatim, the continuous Asp/Glu-rich GH9 neighbourhood in the spliced +1 frame, and essentially identical nucleotide match to published Microcerotermes annadalai GH9 cellulase continue to favour C over D. D concerns about in-frame stops and size remain the main counter-argument but are increasingly attributed to intronic regions in a genomic gene fragment.

